    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d244">

                <pb facs="0366" n="1120" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v02/pg1120"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 855.48/608</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-06-30T11:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 30, 1917, 11 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 6 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6585. Commission asks that following be transmitted to Hoover.</p>
                <p>15. See our 14 to Washington.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d244-f1"/> British Ship
                    Control would be glad if pressure could be put on Dutch in the United States to
                    allocate tonnage to commission. In consideration of generous allotment to us,
                    say 60,000 tons monthly, could Dutch cargoes held in United States be allowed
                    proceed? What are prospects?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
